# NOTE ON ANCHORING: the artefact patterns below are deliberately NOT anchored.
# Every rule in this file once started with `/app/`, back when the mix project
# lived in that subdirectory, which is how 1,308 `.elixir_ls/` paths and 933
# compiled `.beam` files reached the repository history. Build artefacts can
# appear at any depth, so the rules that match them are left unanchored. The
# project now sits at the repository root and the few anchored rules start at
# `/priv/`.

# The directory Mix will write compiled artifacts to.
_build/

# If you run "mix test --cover", coverage assets end up here.
cover/

# The directory Mix downloads your dependencies sources to.
deps/

# Where 3rd-party dependencies like ExDoc output generated docs.
doc/

# Ignore .fetch files in case you like to edit your project deps locally.
.fetch

# If the VM crashes, it generates a dump, let's ignore it too.
erl_crash.dump

# Also ignore archive artifacts (built via "mix archive.build").
*.ez

# Compiled BEAM bytecode, wherever it lands.
*.beam

# ElixirLS build output and Dialyzer PLTs. Never source; these are large
# (a PLT alone is ~6 MB) and were previously committed by accident.
.elixir_ls/
*.plt
*.plt.hash

# Temporary files, for example, from tests.
tmp/

# Ignore package tarball (built via "mix hex.build").
eft_buddy-*.tar

# Ignore assets that are produced by build tools.
/priv/static/assets/

# Ignore digested assets cache.
/priv/static/cache_manifest.json

# `mix phx.digest` — which `mix assets.deploy` runs, and which therefore runs on
# every production build — writes a digested copy AND a .gz variant next to
# EVERY source file throughout priv/static, not only under assets/.
# Committing those would duplicate every image, font and svg in the repo (~100
# files). Remove any that appear with `mix phx.digest.clean --all`.
/priv/static/**/*.gz
/priv/static/**/*-????????????????????????????????.*

# In case you use Node.js/npm, you want to ignore these.
npm-debug.log
node_modules/

# Local env vars (DB_PASSWORD, etc.)
.env

# ── Must never reach the public repository ────────────────────────────────────
#
# `private/` holds the engineering audit: `private/audit/ENGINEERING_DUE_DILIGENCE.md`
# is a prioritised map of every known weakness in this codebase, including a worked
# exploit chain, plus the prompt that produced it.
#
# It lives behind an ignore rule rather than a note-to-self on purpose. The plan for
# going public is a FRESH repository with one squashed commit, i.e. `git init &&
# git add .` — so anything merely "remembered" to be excluded ships. An ignored
# directory is a mechanism; a comment is a hope. Keep audit material in here and
# the publication step cannot leak it.
private/

# The rest are generated / editor-local. These are currently TRACKED in this
# private repository; listing them does not untrack them (git keeps tracking what it
# already tracks) — it stops new churn, and it is the state the fresh public
# repository should start from.
#
#   graphify-out/  generated knowledge-graph dump: 290 files, ~68% of tracked
#                  bytes, and it embeds absolute local paths from the machine
#                  that produced it.
#   .claude/       third-party generated agent config; two of its rule files
#                  describe a Node.js project and do not apply here.
#   .vscode/       editor-local settings.
graphify-out/
.claude/
.vscode/

# Scratch handoff notes get written to the repo root while work is in progress,
# and they are not meant to be committed. Four of them once rode a `git add -A`
# onto public main, carrying the VM's address, the pooler host and an
# outstanding password-rotation note with them.
#
# Ignoring root markdown by default mirrors the .dockerignore rule that already
# keeps these out of the image, so a new scratch file is safe the moment it is
# created rather than only if someone remembers to stage selectively.
/*.md
!/README.md
!/AGENTS.md
